(((window.HALCYON_DATA=window.HALCYON_DATA||{}).cphims=window.HALCYON_DATA.cphims||{}).full=window.HALCYON_DATA.cphims.full||[]).push({
  num: 6, part: `Part Three: One Chart`, title: `The Business Case`, weeks: `Months 5 to 6`,
  tasks: [`SA1`,`SA2`,`SA3`,`SA4`,`SA5`,`SA6`,`SA8`,`SA9`,`C5`,`L19`,`L25`,`E1`],
  cast: [[`Glenn Morrow`,`CFO, Cascade Valley Health. Not hostile, just allergic to soft numbers. Wants NPV, not adjectives.`]],
  opening: [
    `On Thursday, March 4, Maria was on the fourth floor by 6:15, and she still wasn't first. The light was on in the analysts' room. Kiran sat between two monitors with a sleeve of saltines, the way he'd sat most mornings since the sepsis pilot, and the timestamps in his folder said he'd left at one and come back at five.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Kiran">"Ochoa called me last night too,"</span> he said before she could ask. <span class="said" data-who="Kiran">"Right after you. He said you'd need numbers, and I'd know where they lived."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Maria">"Did you eat dinner?"</span>`,
    `He thought about it longer than the question deserved. <span class="said" data-who="Kiran" data-tone="Sheepish, evasive, light.">"I had the crackers."</span>`,
    `At 7:02 an email came in from a man she'd met four times, always across a table with a budget on it.`,
    `<prop type="email" from="Glenn Morrow" to="Maria Santos" time="7:02 AM" subject="One Chart">Maria, Ray says you'll have a business case for the board in thirty days. Glad to help. So neither of us is surprised, here are the three questions I'll ask. One. What does it cost to do nothing? Two. What does it cost if you're wrong? Three. Who owns each benefit after you've moved on to the next thing? I've read a lot of business cases. Most of them answer a fourth question nobody asked, which is how badly everyone wants this. Glenn</prop>`,
    `She took the legal pad out of her bag, the one from the kitchen drawer. Last night's line was at the top. What does it cost to keep MedaLink? Glenn had asked it first, in politer words. She wrote his other two under it and drew a box around all three.`,
    `The board met Friday, April 2. That gave her four weeks to price eighteen months of work at three hospitals she'd spent the winter learning, and to do it without rounding anything up.`,
    `On her way out she put a hand on the back of Kiran's chair. <span class="said" data-who="Maria" data-tone="Warm, firm, no argument.">"Lunch today. A real one."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Kiran" data-tone="Quick, distracted, absent.">"Sure,"</span> he said. He didn't.`
  ],
  lesson: {
    title: `Ochoa's Legal Pad: what it costs, and what it buys`,
    sections: [
      { h: `Where you are in the life cycle`,
        body: [`Sunday morning at the Greeley diner, Ochoa drew a line across the top of the legal pad and put six tick marks on it. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"Everybody wants to start at the fourth mark,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"That's the one where you get to build things."</span>`,
          `The systems development life cycle, SDLC, is the path every system takes. Planning decides whether to do it at all, and why. The business case lives here. Analysis finds out what people actually need. Design decides how the system will meet those needs. Build and test makes it and proves it. Implementation puts it in front of users. Maintenance and support run it until it's retired, which starts the cycle over. Waterfall runs the phases in order. Agile and iterative methods loop through them in small pieces. Either way, skipping a phase doesn't skip its work. It moves the work later, where it costs more.`],
        terms: [[`SDLC`,`Systems development life cycle: planning, analysis, design, build and test, implementation, and maintenance and support.`],[`Business case`,`The document that justifies a project: the problem, the alternatives, the costs and benefits, the risks, and the recommendation.`]],
        exam: `Questions describe an activity and ask which phase it belongs to, or what comes first. Funding and the business case come before requirements, and requirements come before design.` },
      { h: `What they need, and what's missing`,
        body: [`Needs analysis asks the people doing the work what they require. Gap analysis compares the current state with the future state and lists what's missing between them. Requirements turn each gap into something you can test. "Discharge should be easy" isn't a requirement. "A routine discharge takes 41 clicks or fewer, measured in test" is.`,
          `Draw it before you write it. A current state process map shows how the work is done today, workarounds included. A future state map shows how it'll be done after. Swimlanes give each role its own row, so the handoffs show up where the lanes cross. Flowcharts use diamonds for decisions. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"That butcher paper from Laramie is the most expensive document you own,"</span> Ochoa said. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"It took ten years of workarounds to make."</span>`],
        terms: [[`Gap analysis`,`A comparison of the current state with the desired future state that lists what has to change to get there.`],[`Swimlane diagram`,`A process map with a row for each role, so steps and handoffs show who does what.`]],
        exam: `For what an analyst should do first, pick the answer that involves the people who do the work and documents the current state before designing the future one. A good requirement is specific and measurable.` },
      { h: `Fix it now, or fix it with the project`,
        body: [`Not every problem should wait for a big project. DMAIC, from Six Sigma, is Define, Measure, Analyze, Improve, Control. It fits a complex problem with an unclear cause and plenty of data, and it takes months. PDCA, Plan, Do, Check, Act, is a short cycle: try a small change, measure it, then keep it, adjust it, or drop it. Use it when the change is small, local, and safe to test this week.`],
        terms: [[`DMAIC`,`Define, Measure, Analyze, Improve, Control. A structured, data-heavy improvement method from Six Sigma.`],[`PDCA`,`Plan, Do, Check, Act. A short, repeatable cycle for testing small changes.`]],
        exam: `A small, testable change on one unit points to PDCA. A complex problem with an unclear cause points to DMAIC. Both start from a measured baseline and end by holding the gain.` },
      { h: `Every road, one yardstick`,
        body: [`A business case compares alternatives, and doing nothing is always one of them. Price each one over the same period, against the same criteria. Count every cost: licenses, implementation services, hardware, interfaces, training, the overtime to cover nurses while they're in class, and the internal staff time everybody forgets. That multi-year total is the total cost of ownership.`,
          `Know what kind of organization each option has to serve. A critical access hospital is a small rural hospital with no more than 25 inpatient beds, an emergency department open around the clock, and usually no other hospital within 35 miles. Medicare pays it based on its reasonable costs instead of fixed rates, so what it spends, IT included, shapes how it's paid. It isn't a clinic, and an option that treats it like one fails before the math starts.`],
        terms: [[`Total cost of ownership`,`Every cost of an option over its life or a set period, including internal labor, support, and training.`],[`Critical access hospital`,`A small rural hospital, 25 inpatient beds or fewer, with 24-hour emergency care and cost-based Medicare payment.`]],
        exam: `An answer that presents only the preferred option, or compares options over different periods, is wrong. Expect to recognize organization types by their features: beds, services, location, and how they're paid.` },
      { h: `Glenn's arithmetic`,
        body: [`Money now is worth more than money later. Net present value discounts each year's net cash flow by (1 + r) raised to the year, adds them up, and subtracts the initial cost. The rate r is the organization's cost of capital, and the CFO sets it, not the project. A positive NPV means the project earns more than that rate. Payback period is how long until the cumulative net cash flow covers the initial cost. ROI is total benefits minus total costs, divided by total costs.`,
          `Hard benefits are cash you can trace, like a license you stop paying. Soft benefits are real and harder to price, like safer medication passes. Don't turn a soft benefit into dollars with someone else's averages. Report it as a measured outcome with a baseline. Then stress the case. Sensitivity analysis asks what happens to NPV if benefits come in 20 percent low or costs run over. A contingency reserve is money set aside for risks you've named. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"Glenn doesn't need the case to be big,"</span> Ochoa said. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"He needs to know what happens when it's wrong."</span>`],
        terms: [[`Net present value`,`The sum of each year's net cash flow divided by (1 + r) to the power of the year, minus the initial cost.`],[`Payback period`,`The time until cumulative net cash flow equals the initial investment.`],[`Sensitivity analysis`,`Recalculating a result while changing one key assumption, to see how much the answer depends on it.`]],
        exam: `Expect to compute NPV, payback, and ROI from a small table. When an answer inflates benefits or hides a financial risk to win approval, it's wrong, even if the project is a good one.` },
      { h: `Baselines and owners`,
        body: [`Every benefit needs a baseline measured before the change, and it has to be the right one. If something else moves the measure first, like a new staffing model, the project only gets credit for the gap that's left. Compare like with like. Sicker patients, longer trips, or a different season can explain a difference before the system does.`,
          `A benefits realization plan names, for each benefit, the baseline, the target, the measure, the data source, the review dates, and the owner. The owner is the leader whose operation has to deliver it, like a CNO or the revenue cycle director, not IT. Someone checks it after go-live, at six months, a year, and two years.`,
          `When you present it, lead with the recommendation and the decision you need. Show the options, the numbers, and the downside on one page, and walk the CFO through it before the meeting so nobody's surprised in front of the board. Ochoa tore the page off. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"A business case outlives whoever wrote it. Write it for the person who checks."</span>`],
        terms: [[`Benefits realization plan`,`The plan that names each expected benefit's owner, baseline, target, measure, and review dates after go-live.`],[`Baseline`,`The measured level of a metric before a change, used to judge what the change achieved.`]],
        exam: `Benefits are owned by the business, measured against a baseline, and reviewed after go-live. Expect analytics items that ask what else could explain a difference. For a board, lead with the recommendation, show risk honestly, and brief key stakeholders first.` }
    ],
    video: [`GwQvw8y63V8`,`NPV vs. The Payback Method`,`Edspira`,`10:38`]
  },
  scenes: [
    { id:`f6s1`, domain:`Systems Management`, task:`SA1`, title:`The fourth mark`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`By Thursday afternoon Maria knew where Kiran's nights had gone. Since January, between sepsis builds, he'd been building in the Corvane test system: thirty-four order sets for the three Wyoming hospitals, copied from the Corvane hospitals and changed by hand to match MedaLink.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Kiran">"We'll need them anyway,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Kiran">"If I keep going, we're three months ahead when the board says yes. Nobody asked. I just didn't want to be behind."</span>`,`The board hadn't said yes. Nobody had asked the Wyoming nurses what they needed. And Kiran's lunch was still in its bag beside his keyboard.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Let him keep building, since the sets will be needed and it puts One Chart ahead.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-3,health:-3},
          after:`By April he has sixty. Half are built on Fort Collins habits, and when the Wyoming nurses finally see them in June, most get rebuilt. Kiran builds them twice, at night both times.`,
          why:`Building before the decision and before analysis doesn't save the work. It does it on guesses, and the guesses get redone. The life cycle starts with planning and analysis for a reason.`},
        {t:`Adopt Corvane's standard implementation method, phases and all, next week.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`Corvane's method has every phase, nicely drawn. It starts at design, because it assumes the customer has already decided. Maria still has no business case, and now she has a kickoff date.`,
          why:`A vendor's method is a fine template once you've decided. It doesn't do the planning that decides whether, why, and what. Tailor it after the case is approved.`},
        {t:`Pause the build, place One Chart in the life cycle, and use his sets as input.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:5,health:4},
          after:`Maria draws Ochoa's line on Kiran's whiteboard and puts a dot at the first tick. Planning. The business case decides whether there's a project at all. Then analysis, with Wyoming in the room. Kiran's order sets go in a folder for the requirements sessions, where the Laramie nurses can mark them up. He looks at the dot a long time. <span class="said" data-who="Kiran" data-tone="Quiet, deflated, sheepish.">"So I'm early."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Maria" data-tone="Gentle, firm, warm.">"You're early and you're tired. Go home at five."</span> He goes home at seven, which is progress.`,
          why:`Know where you are in the SDLC. A project in planning needs a decision and a business case, then requirements, then design and build. Early work isn't wasted if it feeds analysis instead of skipping it.`},
        {t:`Bring in a contractor to help Kiran build, so he isn't doing it alone at night.`, s:0, d:{trust:1,conf:-2,health:-2},
          after:`The contractor is good and fast. Now two people are building order sets for a project that doesn't exist yet, from requirements nobody has written. Kiran trains the contractor at night.`,
          why:`Helping an overloaded analyst is the right instinct, aimed at the wrong work. The problem isn't the build's speed. It's that the build came before the decision and the analysis.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f6s2`, domain:`Systems Management`, task:`SA2`, title:`In ink`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Monday, March 8. Kiran had laid October's inventory beside Corvane's standard build: every job MedaLink did at the three hospitals, line by line, and whether Corvane did it the same way. Most lines matched. Some didn't. For a few, nobody could say.`,`Walt Hagen called at 7:15, which Renee later said meant he'd been waiting since six. <span class="said" data-who="Walt">"I hear you're writing down what we need,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Walt">"Last time somebody did that, they sent us a survey and built what Fort Collins wanted. What are you doing this time?"</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Use Corvane's standard build as the requirements, and have Wyoming adapt to it.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:-2,health:-2},
          after:`It's fast, and it's what eight hospitals already run. It also assumes Rawlins works like Fort Collins. Nobody notices the two Wyoming state reports MedaLink sends until a state office calls in the fall.`,
          why:`A standard build is a starting point for gap analysis, not a substitute for one. Requirements come from the people and the work the system has to serve.`},
        {t:`Send every department a request for its wish list, then compile what comes back.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`Six hundred and twelve wishes come back, from a new chair in the ED to a dictation tool nobody has priced. None says which ones matter. Walt sends one line. <span class="said" data-who="Walt">"Mine's on page nine."</span>`,
          why:`Asking beats assuming, but a wish list isn't a needs analysis. Needs come from the work, get ranked, and get written so they can be tested.`},
        {t:`Walk the gaps with Wyoming clinicians, and write each one as a testable requirement.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:5,health:4},
          after:`Two weeks of sessions, a day at Cheyenne and Laramie and a long video call for Rawlins. Each MedaLink job, how Corvane does it, what's missing, and a line someone can test. They find three gaps nobody knew about: the two state reports, and Laramie's surgery schedule printing to the floor at 5 AM. Walt gets his own line. <span class="said" data-who="Walt">"Forty-one clicks or fewer for a routine discharge. Measured. Write it down."</span> Maria does, in ink. <span class="said" data-who="Walt">"Huh,"</span> he says.`,
          why:`Needs analysis and gap analysis compare the current state with the future state, with the people who do the work. Each gap becomes a requirement that's specific and testable, so you can prove later whether you met it.`},
        {t:`Have Corvane write the requirements into the contract, since it knows its system.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`Corvane's draft is thorough about Corvane. It says nothing about the 5 AM surgery schedule or the state reports, because nobody at Corvane knew to ask.`,
          why:`The vendor knows its product, not your needs. Requirements belong to the organization and its users, and the vendor responds to them.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f6s3`, domain:`Systems Management`, task:`SA4`, title:`The other wall`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Wednesday Maria drove to Laramie with the photo Renee had taken in January, printed four feet wide. Five lanes: surgeon, clerk, pharmacy, cabinet, nurse. Three places where a dose went around the scanner.`,`Renee had saved the same conference room. Jess came on her break, and the pharmacist from downstairs, and Kiran on a laptop propped against the coffee pot again. Somebody had to draw what One Chart would look like on this floor, and the business case needed to know what it would cost to get there.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Hang Corvane's standard medication workflow beside it as the future state.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`Corvane's diagram is clean and has no Laramie in it. It doesn't show the liquids without barcodes or the night pharmacist who isn't there. Renee studies it. <span class="said" data-who="woman">"That's a nice hospital. Where is it?"</span>`,
          why:`A future state map has to show your future, with your people and your constraints. A vendor's reference workflow is an input, not the map.`},
        {t:`Draw the future state with the same nurses, lane for lane beside the old one.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:5,health:4},
          after:`They draw it on a second roll, lane for lane. Two of the three workarounds disappear in the new lanes. The third doesn't. Corvane can scan a liquid only if it has a barcode, and Laramie's pharmacy has no way to print one. That's a label printer, a pharmacy tech's hours, and a line in the business case Maria didn't know she needed. Jess taps the new lane at the top, Cheyenne's pharmacists. <span class="said" data-who="woman">"So at night somebody actually checks."</span>`,
          why:`Current and future state process maps, drawn with the people who do the work, show what changes, what doesn't, and what the change will need. A gap found on paper is cheaper than one found at go-live.`},
        {t:`Draw the future state with analysts in Fort Collins, then show it to the nurses.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`The analysts draw a good map. The nurses find two mistakes in it in ten minutes, and Renee is polite about it, which is worse.`,
          why:`Close. Reviewing a map catches errors, and drawing it together catches more, because the nurses know which steps really happen.`},
        {t:`Skip the map, since the gap list already covers the medication workflow.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-2},
          after:`The gap list says Corvane does BCMA. It doesn't say Laramie's liquids have no barcodes, because that isn't a missing function. It's a missing step.`,
          why:`A list of functions misses steps, handoffs, and workarounds. Process maps show how the work moves between people. Use both.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f6s4`, domain:`Systems Management`, task:`SA3`, title:`The noon list`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Friday Gail called from Rawlins. Remote verification was twelve nights old, and she'd been watching her own numbers. <span class="said" data-who="Gail">"Nights are better. Days aren't. My day nurses picked up the Given, no scan habit last spring, and they've kept it. I'm not waiting eighteen months for One Chart to fix my day shift."</span>`,`Kiran was on the call, and he had an idea ready, as he always did: a full improvement project, Six Sigma style, with a charter and a black belt from the quality department.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Tell Gail One Chart will fix it, and ask her nurses to hold on until then.`, s:0, d:{trust:-4,conf:-1,health:-3},
          after:`Gail says thank you in a voice that means the opposite. Eighteen more months of day shift doses go around the scanner.`,
          why:`A safety problem you can fix now doesn't wait for a project. Knowing a big fix is coming isn't a reason to leave a small one undone.`},
        {t:`Charter a full DMAIC project with the quality department, as Kiran suggests.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`The black belt is booked until May. The charter takes three weeks. By the time the Measure phase starts, Gail has already tried two things on her own.`,
          why:`DMAIC is right for a complex problem with an unclear cause. This one is small, local, and its cause is known. It needs a quick cycle.`},
        {t:`Help Gail run a small PDCA test on one day shift, measured each week.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`Plan: the charge nurse pulls the Given, no scan list at noon and asks about each one, out loud and kindly. Do: one week on days. Check: the day shift scan rate goes from 70 to 78. Act: keep it, and add evenings. Gail names it the noon list. Maria writes a note on her legal pad for Glenn. Whatever the noon list fixes, One Chart doesn't get credit for.`,
          why:`PDCA fits a small, safe change you can test this week: plan it, do it, check the measure, act on what you learned. And once it works, the baseline your business case uses has to include it.`},
        {t:`Have Kiran remove the Given, no scan button from MedaLink at Rawlins now.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:-2,health:-3},
          after:`The button goes away Monday. On Tuesday a wristband won't scan on a patient in pain, and the nurse has no way to chart the dose she gave. She writes it on paper.`,
          why:`The button exists for real scanning failures. Removing it without a plan for those blocks charting and pushes the workaround onto paper.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f6s5`, domain:`Environments`, task:`E1`, title:`Twenty-two beds`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Tuesday, March 16, Glenn Morrow came to Maria's office at eight, after Ochoa had finished with her, and sat in the chair by the window with her draft and a pencil. He was a slight, tidy man who'd been CFO for nine years and read numbers the way Walt read clicks.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Glenn">"Rawlins,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Glenn">"Twenty-two beds, and it loses money most years. Four million of this is for a building that averages fourteen patients. Why not put it on Corvane's clinic system, the one the forty clinics use? It's a fraction of the cost."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Explain that Rawlins is a critical access hospital, and what that means for it.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:5,health:4},
          after:`Maria gives it to him in order. Twenty-two inpatient beds, some of them swing beds. An emergency room open all night with one doctor, the only one for a hundred miles of I-80. Inpatients need an inpatient record: orders, an eMAR, BCMA. A clinic system has none of that. And Medicare pays Rawlins on its reasonable costs, so part of what One Chart costs there comes back through its cost report. Glenn has stopped writing. <span class="said" data-who="Glenn" data-tone="Quiet, dry, reflective.">"My first job was the cost report for a twenty-bed hospital in western Nebraska."</span> He crosses out a line. <span class="said" data-who="Glenn">"Ask my reimbursement team for that number. Don't guess it."</span>`,
          why:`Know the type of organization and what it does. A critical access hospital is a small rural inpatient hospital with round-the-clock emergency care and cost-based Medicare payment. It needs an inpatient system, and its payment model belongs in the business case.`},
        {t:`Agree, and plan Rawlins on the clinic system to cut that part of the cost.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-3,health:-4},
          after:`The draft gets cheaper by Friday. In April someone asks where Rawlins nurses will chart a heparin drip, and the clinic system has no answer.`,
          why:`A clinic system serves outpatients. A hospital with inpatient beds and an emergency room needs inpatient functions. Savings that remove required functions aren't savings.`},
        {t:`Move Rawlins to a second phase, a year after Cheyenne and Laramie go live.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`It spreads the cost, and Glenn likes that. Then Maria reads the MedaLink letter again. Support ends for all three hospitals on the same day.`,
          why:`Phasing can be smart, but not past a hard deadline. Know the organization's constraints before you spread the work.`},
        {t:`Tell Glenn this is a patient safety decision, and cost shouldn't decide it.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-2,health:0},
          after:`Glenn nods politely and writes something down. It isn't agreement. Rawlins stays in, and Maria has lost her chance to show why it costs what it does.`,
          why:`Safety is the reason, and cost is still the CFO's job. Explain what the hospital is and how it's paid. Refusing to talk about money loses the room.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f6s6`, domain:`Systems Management`, task:`SA6`, title:`Four roads`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Glenn had left one more question in pencil in the margin of page one. Why not pay somebody to keep MedaLink running? Ochoa had asked the same thing in October.`,`Kiran had prices by Wednesday. A third-party support firm would keep MedaLink patched after its vendor walked away. A smaller EHR company had quoted the three hospitals on their own. And Marcus had sent a count: sixty-four Relay interfaces touched MedaLink, and on every road but standing still, each one would be rebuilt or retired.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Compare One Chart with third-party support, the option Glenn asked about.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`It answers Glenn's question and only his. A board member asks about the smaller vendor in the first five minutes of the review, and Maria has no numbers for it.`,
          why:`Answering the CFO's question is right. A business case still has to compare every realistic option, doing nothing included, so the board can see why the winner won.`},
        {t:`Present One Chart alone, since it's the only option that gives one record.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`It's a short case. Glenn sends it back with one word in the margin. Alternatives?`,
          why:`A case with one option isn't a case. Decision makers need to see the alternatives and why they lost.`},
        {t:`Price all four over five years, doing nothing too, on the same criteria.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:6,health:4},
          after:`Four columns, five years, every cost in each. Doing nothing: $16.0 million, and no patches after June 2028. Third-party support: $22.0 million, and still two records. The smaller vendor: $19.0 million, counting a year of MedaLink overlap, and now three records. One Chart: $17.0 million, plus $1.5 million of MedaLink still running in year one, $18.5 million, and one record. Under each, the same rows: safety, the system plan's one-record goal, risk, and what the nurses give up in the change. Glenn reads it. <span class="said" data-who="Glenn">"Doing nothing is the cheapest, and you put it first."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Maria">"It is the cheapest. Until it isn't."</span>`,
          why:`Evaluate alternate solutions side by side: same period, same cost categories, same criteria. Include doing nothing as the baseline. Showing that it's cheaper and still unacceptable makes the case stronger.`},
        {t:`Include the others too, but price them high, so that One Chart stands out.`, s:0, d:{trust:-5,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`Glenn's analysts reprice the smaller vendor in an afternoon and find $3 million that Maria added. After that, he checks every number twice.`,
          why:`Weighting the alternatives toward the answer you want is dishonest, and a CFO will find it. Price every option the same way.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f6s7`, domain:`Clinical Informatics`, task:`C5`, title:`Like with like`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Aisha sent the transfer numbers Friday with three exclamation points. Kiran had pulled every 2026 transfer from the three Wyoming hospitals to Greeley and Fort Collins, 2,000 of them. Thirty-eight percent had the same labs or scans repeated within six hours of arrival. For transfers between Corvane hospitals, it was fifteen percent.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Aisha">"Twenty-three points,"</span> she wrote. <span class="said" data-who="Aisha">"That's the record. The receiving doctor can't see what Wyoming already did, so they do it again. Put it in."</span>`,`At $2,500 a repeat workup, twenty-three points on 2,000 transfers came to $1.15 million a year.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Put the full twenty-three points in, since the record gap explains it.`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:-3,health:-1},
          after:`It's in the draft by noon. Glenn's analyst asks how long the Wyoming patients rode in the ambulance first. Nobody knows, and the line comes out of the case entirely.`,
          why:`A difference between two groups isn't all caused by the thing you're proposing. Rule out other explanations before you put a number in front of a CFO.`},
        {t:`Compare like with like, matching diagnosis and the hours since the last draw.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:6,health:3},
          after:`Kiran matches them overnight, though Maria didn't ask him to do it overnight. The Wyoming patients were sicker, and most rode two to three hours, long enough that some labs needed repeating anyway. Matched, the gap is ten points. Aisha has two ED doctors read fifty of the repeats, and most of the ten were tests nobody in Greeley could see. Ten points on 2,000 transfers is 200 workups, $500,000 a year. Aisha frowns. <span class="said" data-who="Aisha">"Smaller."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Maria">"And Glenn will believe it."</span>`,
          why:`Interpreting outcomes means asking what else could explain a difference. Adjust for case mix and timing, check the result with clinicians, and put the number that survives in the case.`},
        {t:`Use half the gap, about twelve points, so the number is safely conservative.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:-1,health:0},
          after:`It's conservative, and it's a guess. When Glenn asks where twelve came from, the honest answer is that it seemed safe.`,
          why:`Cutting a number by a round fraction feels careful, but it isn't analysis. Find out what the gap really is.`},
        {t:`Leave the transfers out of the case, since the data are too messy to trust.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`The case loses a real benefit that receiving doctors see every week. Aisha says so at council, with two of them nodding.`,
          why:`Messy data need cleaning and adjusting, not discarding. A benefit you can measure honestly belongs in the case.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f6s8`, domain:`Clinical Informatics`, task:`C5`, title:`The new baseline`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Monday morning Kiran sent the Rawlins nights, week by week.`,
        `<prop type="chart" kind="bars" title="Rawlins BCMA scan compliance, night shift, by week" labels="Jan 4,Jan 11,Jan 18,Jan 25,Feb 1,Feb 8,Feb 15,Feb 22,Mar 1,Mar 8,Mar 15" values="61,63,62,60,62,63,61,62,71,76,80">Percent of night doses scanned. Eight weeks before remote verification started on March 1, then three weeks after. The median of the eight baseline weeks is 62.</prop>`,
        `Aisha's draft benefit was already in the file. One Chart brings Rawlins scan compliance from 62 percent to 95. Glenn had circled the 62.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Keep 62 as the baseline, since that's where Rawlins was when the case began.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-3,health:-1},
          after:`The case claims thirty-three points at Rawlins. By summer the nights are in the mid eighties with MedaLink still running, and Glenn's analyst asks which of the thirty-three points One Chart is going to deliver.`,
          why:`A baseline has to reflect the process as it is when the project starts. If another change has already moved the measure, the project can't claim that gain.`},
        {t:`Use 80, this week's number, as the new baseline for the Rawlins nights.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`It's closer to the truth. It's also one point, three weeks into a change, and it might keep climbing or slide back.`,
          why:`Right direction. A single recent point isn't a stable baseline. Let it settle, or use a range.`},
        {t:`Credit telepharmacy, let the new level settle, and claim only the gap left.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:6,health:4},
          after:`Maria rewrites the line. Rawlins nights rose the week Cheyenne started verifying, and all three weeks since sit above every baseline week. That gain belongs to telepharmacy, and the day shift's belongs to the noon list. The case will use the new level once it holds for six weeks, and One Chart claims only what's left: scans MedaLink can't support, like liquids without barcodes. She sends it to Aisha first. Aisha writes back one word. <span class="said" data-who="Aisha">"Fair."</span> Then a second message. <span class="said" data-who="Aisha">"Annoyingly."</span>`,
          why:`Read a measure over time against its own baseline, and credit the change to what actually changed. When something else moves the measure first, re-baseline once it's stable and claim only the gap that's left.`},
        {t:`Drop BCMA from the case, since telepharmacy has already fixed the problem.`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:-2,health:-3},
          after:`The case gets simpler. It also stops mentioning the liquids, the bands that won't read, and Laramie at 81, none of which telepharmacy touched.`,
          why:`One fix closing part of a gap doesn't close all of it. Measure what's left before you drop a benefit.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f6s9`, domain:`Systems Management`, task:`SA8`, title:`The calculator`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Wednesday Aisha forwarded an email with a spreadsheet attached and a note on top.`,
        `<prop type="email" from="Brett Talbot" to="Aisha Rahman" time="4:12 PM" subject="One Chart ROI">Aisha, here's the Corvane value calculator our customers use for board cases. Plug in your beds and it does the rest. Medication safety alone comes out around $2.4 million a year for three hospitals your size. Most boards like that line.</prop>`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Aisha">"He's selling, I know,"</span> she wrote. <span class="said" data-who="Aisha">"But safety is the real reason we're doing this, and right now it's worth zero dollars in your case. Glenn only reads dollars."</span>`,`With the calculator's line, the NPV went from just under six hundred thousand dollars to about ten million.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Use the calculator's $2.4 million, since safety is why we're doing this.`, s:0, flag:`softroi`, d:{trust:-2,conf:-4,health:-2},
          after:`The NPV jumps to about $10 million, and the case looks easy. The $2.4 million rests on Corvane's averages from other hospitals, and on page six, in small type, the spreadsheet says so.`,
          why:`Turning a soft benefit into dollars with a vendor's averages rounds the case up. It may win the vote and it'll lose the review, because nobody at Cascade can measure it.`},
        {t:`Leave safety out of the case entirely, so that every number in it is hard.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:1,health:-1},
          after:`Every number holds. The board reads a case about invoices and denials, and nobody on it hears about Rawlins at 62 until the next quality committee.`,
          why:`Hard numbers are right for NPV. A case that leaves out its main reason misleads in the other direction. Show safety, measured.`},
        {t:`Include half the calculator's figure, $1.2 million, as a careful middle.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-3,health:-1},
          after:`Half of a number nobody can check is still a number nobody can check. Glenn asks where it came from, and the answer is Brett, divided by two.`,
          why:`Discounting an unsupported estimate doesn't make it supported. Measure it locally or present it as a non-financial benefit.`},
        {t:`Keep NPV to hard dollars, and show safety as measured outcomes instead.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:6,health:5},
          after:`Two parts. On page one, the NPV, built on dollars Cascade can trace. Right under it, a box: what One Chart buys that isn't priced. Scan compliance by hospital, against the new baselines. Medication errors reported. Transfer med lists that arrive complete. Each measured now, each with a target. Aisha stops on the box. <span class="said" data-who="Aisha">"It's not dollars."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Maria">"It's patients. He reads those too."</span>`,
          why:`In cost-benefit analysis, keep hard and soft benefits apart. Price only what you can trace. Present the rest as measured outcomes with baselines and owners, so they're visible without inflating the math.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f6s10`, domain:`Leadership`, task:`L25`, title:`Question two`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Friday Glenn went through the draft line by line in his own office, with his reimbursement director on speaker and the door closed. He checked the discount rate, his 8 percent. He checked the MedaLink invoices, and the overtime to cover nurses while they trained.`,
        ((s.flags.softroi||G.softroi)
          ?`Then he reached page one and set the pencil down. <span class="said" data-who="Glenn" data-tone="Flat, cold, exacting.">"Ten million in NPV, and two point four of it every year comes from a vendor's spreadsheet. I'm taking it out."</span> He did, while she watched. <span class="said" data-who="Glenn">"What's left is $586,000 on twelve million. That's thin. So here's my question two. What does it cost if you're wrong?"</span>`
          :`He didn't find anything to change, and that seemed to worry him more than if he had. <span class="said" data-who="Glenn">"$586,000 of NPV on twelve million,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Glenn">"That's thin. So here's my question two. What does it cost if you're wrong?"</span>`)],
      opts:[
        {t:`Quietly add 10 percent to every cost line, so the project comes in under budget.`, s:0, d:{trust:-4,conf:-3,health:-1},
          after:`The NPV goes negative on paper. Glenn's analyst finds the padding in an afternoon, and after that every line in the case is a negotiation.`,
          why:`Hidden padding makes every estimate untrustworthy and hides risk instead of managing it. Name your contingency and show what it covers.`},
        {t:`Show the downside: named risks, the contingency, and a 20 percent miss.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:6,health:4},
          after:`Maria shows him three numbers. The $12.0 million already holds a $1.2 million contingency for named risks, like backfill rates and data conversion. If benefits come in 20 percent low, NPV is minus $2.7 million. If the up-front cost runs 15 percent over, minus $1.2 million. And one risk money can't fix: MedaLink support ends in June 2028 whether Cascade is ready or not. Glenn turns the page back and reads it again. <span class="said" data-who="Glenn" data-tone="Flat, measured, unimpressed.">"It's thin."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Maria" data-tone="Steady, plain, confident.">"It's thin and it's true. I can defend that."</span>`,
          why:`Financial risk belongs in the open. Name the risks, size a contingency for them, and run sensitivity on the biggest drivers, so decision makers see how the case fails as well as how it succeeds.`},
        {t:`Add a flat 10 percent contingency, and present the base case on its own.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`Glenn accepts the contingency. Then he asks what happens if the denials work doesn't land, and the case has no answer.`,
          why:`A contingency helps, but a flat percentage isn't tied to named risks, and a base case alone hides how sensitive it is. Show the range.`},
        {t:`Tell Glenn the estimates are conservative, so the risk is already covered.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-2,health:0},
          after:`Glenn has heard that sentence in every business case he's read. He writes it down, which isn't a compliment.`,
          why:`Calling the numbers conservative isn't risk analysis. Show the scenarios and what each does to the result.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f6s11`, domain:`Systems Management`, task:`SA9`, title:`Whose number`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Tuesday at 7:30 Ochoa read the draft at his desk with a red pen he didn't use. Then he turned to the page Glenn's third question had built: the benefits.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"Five of them,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"MedaLink costs retired. Denials. Repeat workups on transfer. Scan compliance. Walt's clicks. Who's going to check, in 2029, whether any of them happened?"</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Name Maria's team as the owner of all five, since her team runs the project.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`Her team will measure them faithfully. Nobody in revenue cycle or nursing feels they're theirs, and when denials don't drop, the revenue cycle director calls it an IT problem.`,
          why:`IT can measure, but the business owns the benefit. The leader whose operation has to change is the one who can deliver it.`},
        {t:`Measure each benefit at go-live, then close the plan with the project.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-3,health:-1},
          after:`At go-live most of the benefits haven't started. The plan closes on the one day it can only show costs.`,
          why:`Benefits arrive after go-live, often months after. The plan has to keep measuring at set points after the project ends.`},
        {t:`Give each one a business owner, a baseline, a target, and review dates.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:6,health:4},
          after:`One table. MedaLink costs: Glenn's finance team, against the 2026 invoices, gone by the end of 2028. Denials: the Wyoming revenue cycle director. Repeat workups: the chief medical officers at Greeley and Fort Collins, where the transfers land. Scan compliance: each hospital's CNO, Gail included. Clicks: Walt, who asked for it. Each with its measure and data source, reviewed at six, twelve, and twenty-four months by the board's finance committee. Ochoa caps the pen. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa" data-tone="Quiet, reflective, measured.">"Somebody will be in this chair in 2029,"</span> he says. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"Now they'll know who to call."</span>`,
          why:`A benefits realization plan names each benefit's owner in the business, its baseline, target, measure, data source, and when it's reviewed after go-live. It keeps the case accountable after the project team moves on.`},
        {t:`List a target for each, and let the owners set baselines after go-live.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-3,health:-1},
          after:`Baselines measured after go-live include go-live's chaos. Every benefit looks bigger than it is, and nobody can prove which part is real.`,
          why:`Baselines have to be measured before the change. A baseline taken after go-live can't show what the system changed.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f6s12`, domain:`Leadership`, task:`L19`, title:`Twelve minutes`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`The board met Friday afternoon, April 2, on the sixth floor. One Chart had twelve minutes on the agenda, between the audit report and the pension plan.`,`Maria had a sixty-page appendix, four weeks of work, and a one-page summary. Glenn had read all of it. Aisha would be in the room. So would eleven board members who'd last heard about Rawlins the night they learned its numbers had been wrong for a year.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Walk the board through all sixty pages quickly, so they see how thorough it is.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-3,health:0},
          after:`She's on page thirty when the chair asks her to skip to the cost. The recommendation comes in the last ninety seconds, after the questions have started.`,
          why:`Boards decide on a recommendation, not a tour. Thoroughness belongs in the appendix. Lead with what you need from them.`},
        {t:`Ask Ochoa to present it for her, since he's the one the board knows best.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:-1,health:0},
          after:`Ochoa does it well. When a board member asks about the transfer numbers, he turns to Maria, and she answers from a chair along the wall.`,
          why:`The CIO's sponsorship helps. But the person who built the numbers should present them and answer for them.`},
        {t:`Brief Glenn and Aisha first, then lead with the ask, on one page, risks shown.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:6,conf:6,health:4},
          after:`Glenn and Aisha each walked through the page on Wednesday, so nothing in it surprises them, and Glenn says so out loud. Maria starts with the ask: approve One Chart, $12.0 million, go-live in October 2027. Then the four roads, the NPV with its downside, the safety box, and who owns each benefit. She leaves four minutes for questions. A board member asks what happens if it goes badly. Glenn answers before she can. <span class="said" data-who="Glenn" data-tone="Dry, firm, quietly backing her.">"It's on the page. She put it there. I checked."</span>`,
          why:`Present to executives with the recommendation and the decision first, the evidence on one page, and the risks in plain view. Brief the key stakeholders before the meeting so nobody is surprised in front of the board.`},
        {t:`Open with Brett's calculator slide, since boards respond to big numbers.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:-3,health:-1},
          after:`The board likes the slide. Glenn doesn't, and says so, in front of them. The rest of the twelve minutes is about whose numbers to believe.`,
          why:`Leading with a vendor's numbers in your own case invites the question of whose case it is. Present numbers you can defend.`}
      ]}
  ],
  drills: [
    { after:`f6s9`, title:`Net present value of One Chart`, setup:[`The hard-dollar case. One Chart costs $12.0 million up front, in year 0, including a $1.2 million contingency. After that, Corvane's added license and support for the three hospitals costs $1.0 million a year.`,`Benefits are the MedaLink costs retired ($3.2 million a year), fewer claim denials ($0.8 million a year), and fewer repeat workups on transfer ($0.5 million a year), for $4.5 million a year from year 2. Year 1 is a partial year: MedaLink still runs for part of it, so only $1.7 million of its $3.2 million is retired, and benefits total $3.0 million.`,`Glenn's discount rate is 8 percent. NPV = the sum of each year's net cash flow / (1 + r)^t, minus the initial cost. Work in thousands of dollars and round to the nearest thousand. The table is in thousands.`],
      table:[[`Year`,`Benefits`,`Costs`,`Net`],[`0`,`0`,`12,000`,`minus 12,000`],[`1`,`3,000`,`1,000`,`2,000`],[`2`,`4,500`,`1,000`,`3,500`],[`3`,`4,500`,`1,000`,`3,500`],[`4`,`4,500`,`1,000`,`3,500`],[`5`,`4,500`,`1,000`,`3,500`]],
      fields:[{label:`Present value of year 1's net cash flow, thousands`, answer:1852, tol:1},{label:`Total present value of years 1 to 5, thousands`, answer:12586, tol:1},{label:`NPV, thousands`, answer:586, tol:1}],
      solution:[`Present value of each year = net cash flow / 1.08 to the power of the year.`,`Year 1: 2,000 / 1.08 = 1,851.85, or 1,852.`,`Year 2: 3,500 / 1.1664 = 3,000.69. Year 3: 3,500 / 1.259712 = 2,778.41. Year 4: 3,500 / 1.360489 = 2,572.60. Year 5: 3,500 / 1.469328 = 2,382.04.`,`Total present value: 1,851.85 + 3,000.69 + 2,778.41 + 2,572.60 + 2,382.04 = 12,585.59, or 12,586.`,`NPV = 12,585.59 minus 12,000 = 585.59, or $586,000. It's positive, so One Chart earns more than Glenn's 8 percent, but not by much. Adding the calculator's $2.4 million a year would have pushed NPV to about $10 million on a number nobody at Cascade could measure.`] },
    { after:`f6s10`, title:`Payback, ROI, and the downside`, setup:[`Same cash flows as the last drill: minus 12,000 in year 0, then 2,000, 3,500, 3,500, 3,500, and 3,500 (thousands). Five-year benefits total 21,000 and five-year costs total 17,000, counting year 0.`,`Payback period = the years until cumulative net cash flow reaches zero, counting the last year as a fraction. ROI = (total benefits minus total costs) / total costs. Round to two decimals.`,`Glenn's downside case: benefits come in 20 percent low every year, and costs don't change. Recalculate NPV at 8 percent, in thousands, rounded to the nearest thousand.`],
      fields:[{label:`Payback period, years`, answer:3.86, tol:0.01},{label:`Five-year ROI, percent`, answer:23.53, tol:0.01},{label:`NPV if benefits fall 20 percent, thousands`, answer:-2730, tol:1}],
      solution:[`Cumulative net cash flow: minus 12,000 after year 0, minus 10,000 after year 1, minus 6,500 after year 2, minus 3,000 after year 3, plus 500 after year 4.`,`Payback falls in year 4. It needs 3,000 of year 4's 3,500: 3,000 / 3,500 = 0.857. Payback = 3 + 0.857 = 3.86 years.`,`ROI: benefits 3,000 + (4 x 4,500) = 21,000. Costs 12,000 + (5 x 1,000) = 17,000. (21,000 minus 17,000) / 17,000 = 4,000 / 17,000 = 0.23529, or 23.53 percent.`,`Downside: benefits become 2,400 in year 1 and 3,600 after. Net cash flow becomes 1,400, then 2,600 a year.`,`Present values: 1,400 / 1.08 = 1,296.30. 2,600 / 1.1664 = 2,229.08. 2,600 / 1.259712 = 2,063.96. 2,600 / 1.360489 = 1,911.08. 2,600 / 1.469328 = 1,769.52. Total 9,269.94.`,`NPV = 9,269.94 minus 12,000 = minus 2,730.06, or minus $2,730,000. A 20 percent miss on benefits turns the case negative. That's why the case shows it, and why each benefit has an owner.`] }
  ],
  exercise: { after:`f6s1`, title:`Which phase of the life cycle?`, intro:`Every system moves through the same phases. Pick the phase each activity belongs to.`,
    items:[
      {prompt:`Writing the business case and deciding whether the board should fund One Chart.`, choices:[`Planning`,`Analysis`,`Design`,`Build and test`,`Implementation`,`Maintenance and support`], answer:0, why:`Planning decides whether to do the project and why. The business case is a planning product.`},
      {prompt:`Sitting with Wyoming clinicians to list each gap between MedaLink and Corvane.`, choices:[`Planning`,`Analysis`,`Design`,`Build and test`,`Implementation`,`Maintenance and support`], answer:1, why:`Analysis finds out what users need and what's missing. Gap analysis and requirements live here.`},
      {prompt:`Writing the requirement "a routine discharge takes 41 clicks or fewer, measured in test."`, choices:[`Planning`,`Analysis`,`Design`,`Build and test`,`Implementation`,`Maintenance and support`], answer:1, why:`Defining a measurable requirement is analysis. Design decides how the system will meet it.`},
      {prompt:`Deciding which screens, interfaces, and work queues will carry Rawlins night orders to Cheyenne's pharmacists.`, choices:[`Planning`,`Analysis`,`Design`,`Build and test`,`Implementation`,`Maintenance and support`], answer:2, why:`Design specifies how the system will meet the requirements: screens, data flows, and interfaces.`},
      {prompt:`Configuring the Wyoming order sets and running integrated test scripts against them.`, choices:[`Planning`,`Analysis`,`Design`,`Build and test`,`Implementation`,`Maintenance and support`], answer:3, why:`Building the configuration and proving it works is build and test.`},
      {prompt:`The cutover weekend, with superusers at the elbow on every unit.`, choices:[`Planning`,`Analysis`,`Design`,`Build and test`,`Implementation`,`Maintenance and support`], answer:4, why:`Cutover, go-live, and at-the-elbow support during it are implementation.`},
      {prompt:`Applying the first upgrade six months after go-live and watching help desk trends.`, choices:[`Planning`,`Analysis`,`Design`,`Build and test`,`Implementation`,`Maintenance and support`], answer:5, why:`Upgrades, fixes, and ongoing support after go-live are maintenance and support.`}
    ]},
  quiz: [
    {q:`In the systems development life cycle, the business case and the decision to fund a project belong to which phase?`, opts:[`Planning`,`Design`,`Testing`,`Maintenance`], a:0, why:`Planning decides whether and why to do a project. The business case and funding decision come before analysis, design, build, and testing.`, task:`SA1`, domain:`Systems Management`, level:`RE`},
    {q:`A team works through Define, Measure, Analyze, Improve, and Control. Which method is it using?`, opts:[`Plan, do, check, act`,`DMAIC from Six Sigma`,`A root cause analysis`,`A failure mode review`], a:1, why:`DMAIC is the Six Sigma method: Define, Measure, Analyze, Improve, Control. PDCA is the shorter Plan, Do, Check, Act cycle.`, task:`SA3`, domain:`Systems Management`, level:`RE`},
    {q:`Which BEST describes a critical access hospital?`, opts:[`A large center that takes rural transfers`,`A clinic that offers urgent care only`,`A small rural hospital with 25 or fewer beds`,`A hospital with the top trauma designation`], a:2, why:`Critical access hospitals are small rural hospitals with no more than 25 inpatient beds, 24-hour emergency care, and cost-based Medicare payment.`, task:`E1`, domain:`Environments`, level:`RE`},
    {q:`During an EHR migration, a gap analysis compares:`, opts:[`Two vendors' prices with the approved budget`,`Staff skills with the job descriptions for each role`,`Test results with the expected results in the scripts`,`The current state with the desired future state`], a:3, why:`Gap analysis compares where you are with where you need to be and lists what has to change. The others are budgeting, competency review, and testing.`, task:`SA2`, domain:`Systems Management`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`A surgeon says the new system must not make discharges slower. Which is the BEST way to write this as a requirement?`, opts:[`Discharge should be fast and easy for surgeons`,`Routine discharge takes 41 clicks or fewer`,`The system should improve discharge workflow`,`Surgeons will approve the discharge screens`], a:1, why:`A good requirement is specific, measurable, and testable. Clicks for a routine discharge can be counted in test. The others are goals or approvals, not testable requirements.`, task:`SA2`, domain:`Systems Management`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`Before redesigning a medication workflow, an analyst wants to show nurses where each workaround happens today, step by step and by role. Which tool fits BEST?`, opts:[`A Gantt chart of the project schedule`,`A fishbone diagram of possible causes`,`A pie chart of medication error types`,`A swimlane map of the current state`], a:3, why:`A swimlane process map shows each step by role, including handoffs and workarounds. A fishbone sorts causes, a Gantt chart shows schedule, and a pie chart shows proportions.`, task:`SA4`, domain:`Systems Management`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`A legacy EHR loses vendor support in 20 months. Which set of alternatives should the business case compare?`, opts:[`The preferred replacement and a weaker one`,`Each realistic option, plus doing nothing`,`The two options cheapest in the first year`,`Only the options the current vendor offers`], a:1, why:`A business case compares every realistic alternative over the same period and criteria, with doing nothing as the baseline, so decision makers can see why the recommendation wins.`, task:`SA6`, domain:`Systems Management`, level:`AN`},
    {q:`A project costs $1,000,000 now and returns $600,000 a year for two years. At a 10 percent discount rate, what is its NPV, to the nearest thousand?`, opts:[`$200,000`,`$91,000`,`$41,000`,`$33,000`], a:2, why:`600,000 / 1.10 = 545,455. 600,000 / 1.21 = 495,868. Sum 1,041,322. NPV = 1,041,322 minus 1,000,000 = 41,322, or about $41,000. $200,000 ignores discounting, and $91,000 discounts both years by only one year.`, task:`SA8`, domain:`Systems Management`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`Over five years, a system's benefits total $9 million and its costs total $6 million. What is its ROI?`, opts:[`33 percent`,`67 percent`,`50 percent`,`150 percent`], a:2, why:`ROI = (total benefits minus total costs) / total costs = (9 minus 6) / 6 = 0.50, or 50 percent.`, task:`SA8`, domain:`Systems Management`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`In a benefits realization plan, who should own the benefit "fewer claim denials at the three migrating hospitals"?`, opts:[`The revenue cycle director`,`The project's lead analyst`,`The vendor's account team`,`The IT service desk lead`], a:0, why:`The business leader whose operation delivers the benefit owns it. IT and the vendor help measure and enable it, but they can't make denials fall.`, task:`SA9`, domain:`Systems Management`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`Using the HIE's transfer log and each hospital's lab system, an analyst finds that rural transfer patients get more repeat lab tests on arrival than urban transfer patients. Before crediting a shared record with the whole difference, what should the analyst do FIRST?`, opts:[`Report the full difference as the benefit`,`Cut the difference in half to be careful`,`Drop the measure because data are messy`,`Compare similar patients and timing`], a:3, why:`A difference between groups can come from case mix, travel time, or other factors. Compare like with like before attributing it to the system, then confirm with clinicians. Alongside that, check that both sources count a repeat test and the time window the same way.`, task:`SA5`, domain:`Systems Management`, level:`AN`},
    {q:`Two problems reach the quality team. One unit wants to try a new handoff step next week and see if it helps. Claim denials at three hospitals have many possible causes and years of data behind them. Which approach fits BEST?`, opts:[`DMAIC for both, so the method is consistent`,`PDCA for both, so each change moves quickly`,`PDCA for the handoff, DMAIC for the denials`,`A new analytics tool to study both problems`], a:2, why:`PDCA fits a small, local change that's safe to test this week: plan it, try it, check it, then keep, adjust, or drop it. DMAIC fits a complex problem with an unclear cause and plenty of data, and it takes months. One method for both either slows the easy fix or rushes the hard one, and a software tool isn't a method.`, task:`SA3`, domain:`Systems Management`, level:`AN`},
    {q:`A business case's NPV turns negative if benefits come in 20 percent below plan. What should the IT leader do?`, opts:[`Show the board the downside and named risks`,`Add hidden padding so costs come in under`,`Present the base case, since it's positive`,`Raise the benefits until the downside holds`], a:0, why:`Financial risk belongs in the open. Sensitivity analysis and a contingency tied to named risks let the board decide knowing how the case can fail. Padding and inflated benefits hide it.`, task:`L25`, domain:`Leadership`, level:`AN`},
    {q:`An IT leader has twelve minutes to ask the board to fund a project. What should come FIRST in the presentation?`, opts:[`The project's history and team members`,`The recommendation and the decision`,`A detailed review of every cost line`,`The vendor's case studies from other sites`], a:1, why:`Executives need the recommendation and the decision asked of them first, then the evidence and the risks. Detail belongs in the appendix.`, task:`L19`, domain:`Leadership`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`The week before presenting a business case to the board, what should the IT leader do?`, opts:[`Send the full appendix to every member`,`Ask the vendor to co-present the case`,`Ask the CEO to present it in their place`,`Walk the CFO through the numbers first`], a:3, why:`Briefing key stakeholders like the CFO before the meeting surfaces questions early and avoids surprises in front of the board. The leader who built the case should still present it.`, task:`L19`, domain:`Leadership`, level:`AN`},
    {q:`When a project claims a benefit, its baseline is BEST described as the:`, opts:[`Measure taken before the change`,`Target the project has promised`,`National benchmark for the measure`,`Best result since go-live`], a:0, why:`A baseline is the level measured before the change, the starting point every benefit is judged against. A target is where you hope to land, a benchmark is someone else's number, and one good result after go-live isn't a baseline at all.`, task:`C5`, domain:`Clinical Informatics`, level:`RE`},
    {q:`A new medication workflow went live in October. December's adverse drug events are 20 percent above September's. Before calling the workflow a failure, what should the analyst compare FIRST?`, opts:[`The units that reported the most events`,`The vendor's results at other hospitals`,`The same months in the prior year`,`A national rate for adverse drug events`], a:2, why:`Compare like with like. Winter brings sicker patients and flu season, so September to December can rise without the workflow doing anything. The same months last year show whether this is the usual seasonal climb, and plotting the measure over time comes next. Other hospitals and national rates aren't your patients, and a unit breakdown comes after you know the change is real.`, task:`C5`, domain:`Clinical Informatics`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`Two EHR options are compared for a 22-bed rural hospital with an emergency department open around the clock. Option A costs less because it runs on the vendor's clinic platform. Which consideration MOST affects the choice?`, opts:[`Which option has the lower first-year price`,`Whether it supports inpatient and ED care`,`Which option the vendor recommends most`,`Whether staff already know the clinic system`], a:1, why:`This is a critical access hospital: inpatient beds and a 24-hour emergency department, paid by Medicare on its reasonable costs. An option built for a clinic fails before price matters, because it can't run the services the hospital must provide. Price, vendor preference, and familiarity only count among options that fit.`, task:`E1`, domain:`Environments`, level:`AN`},
  ],
  closing:(s,all,G)=>[
    `The board voted at 4:20. Eleven yes, no abstentions. The chair asked one more question after the vote, and it wasn't about money.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="man" data-tone="Quiet, serious, deliberate.">"Rawlins,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="man">"Is it better today than it was in February?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Maria" data-tone="Honest, steady, plain.">"Nights are,"</span> Maria said. <span class="said" data-who="Maria" data-tone="Honest, steady, plain.">"Days are getting there. The rest is what you just bought."</span>`,
    `Then the chair turned a page. The board wanted the implementation partner and the integration platform bid out competitively, with a recommendation by summer. <span class="said" data-who="man">"Ms. Santos will run that?"</span> Ochoa said she would. Nobody had asked her, which she was learning was how it worked.`,
    ((s.flags.softroi||G.softroi)
      ?`Glenn walked her to the elevator. <span class="said" data-who="Glenn">"Good case. Better than the first draft."</span> He pushed the button. <span class="said" data-who="Glenn">"I kept the first draft, the one with Brett's spreadsheet in it. In a year I'll want to talk about why it was there."</span>`
      :`Glenn walked her to the elevator. <span class="said" data-who="Glenn" data-tone="Dry, understated, approving.">"Nine years,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Glenn">"First business case I didn't have to take apart."</span> He pushed the button. <span class="said" data-who="Glenn" data-tone="Dry, quiet, warning.">"Don't let the bids make a liar of it."</span>`),
    (s.score>=30
      ?`It had been a month of making numbers smaller. Twenty-three points had become ten. Sixty-two percent had become a baseline that wasn't hers to claim. Ten million had stayed in a vendor's spreadsheet. What was left was a case Glenn could defend and the board could check, with a name beside every promise. It was less than she'd hoped for and more than she'd had, and it would still be true in 2029.`
      :`It had been a month she'd want some of back. The case had passed, and Glenn's pencil had made it honest about as often as she had. Some of it was hers. Some of it was Walt's clicks, Gail's noon list, and Kiran's nights. Ochoa would ask which was which.`),
    `She found Kiran in the analysts' room at 6:30, the only light on the floor, already starting a requirements list for the bids. That morning the cafeteria cashier had stopped Maria to ask whether the young man who used to come in every day at 12:15 was all right. She hadn't had an answer.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Maria" data-tone="Gentle, firm.">"Go home,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="Kiran">"After the RFP."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Maria">"There is no RFP. The board voted two hours ago."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Kiran" data-tone="Quiet, wry, almost smiling.">"Then I'm early,"</span> he said, and almost smiled.`,
    `He stayed. Maria went back to her office, found Monday's page, wrote KIRAN across the top in capitals, and underlined it twice.`,
    `Home by nine. Lucia had Maria's laptop open on the kitchen table, a bowl of cereal next to the keyboard, both against house rules. The one-page summary was on the screen. <span class="said" data-who="Lucia">"This is the thing? The whole month?"</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Maria">"Sixty more pages in the back. Don't drip milk on it."</span>`,
    `Lucia scrolled down and back up. <span class="said" data-who="Lucia">"Which part's the nurses?"</span> Maria leaned over her and put a finger on the box under the NPV, the Rawlins line. Nights, 62, then the arrow. <span class="said" data-who="Lucia">"That's one number."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Maria" data-tone="Quiet, intent, serious.">"That's every nurse in Rawlins at three in the morning, pulling a dose with nobody to call."</span>`,
    `Lucia chewed for a while. <span class="said" data-who="Lucia" data-tone="Curious, sly, testing.">"Did you round it up?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Maria" data-tone="Quick, light, proud.">"Nope."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Lucia" data-tone="Quiet, impressed, offhand.">"Huh. Okay."</span> Then she took her cereal and went up to bed.`,
    `Maria's phone buzzed at 10:05. Brett Talbot. Subject: Congrats!! Two exclamation points this time. Corvane would love to help with the bids, and was she free for dinner next week, his treat. She didn't open it. It would keep until Monday. So would Brett.`
  ],
  episode:{src:`audio/full-ch06.mp3`, len:`about 35 minutes`},
  next:`The Selection`
});
